{
 "node": "GeoPositionInterpolator",
 "mode": "events",
 "note": "X_ITE 16.6.0 interpolates along a great circle about the earth's centre (the GeoOrigin point when there is one) with the radius changed linearly: the middle of (0, 0, 0) to (0, 90, 0) is (0, 45, 0); the middle of latitude 0 to latitude 60 is latitude 30.0833 and 2.66 km BELOW the ellipsoid; a key equal to the previous key makes a step (the later value wins at the key); a fraction outside the keys clamps; a short keyValue list is padded with its last value; before any input both outputs hold the first key value. value_changed is SFVec3f (32 bit), so it is compared to 1 m at 6.4e6; geovalue_changed is double.",
 "gaps": [
  "when a key sits on the GeoOrigin its local vector is zero and both engines give the same odd point (lon 7.04, 88.5 km below the ellipsoid at the middle of 0 to 20 degrees): the case is kept because the two agree",
  "the great-circle path pivoting on the GeoOrigin is X_ITE's own choice (a defect against any sane reading); the bridge copies it so the numbers agree",
  "the interpolation is not linear in latitude, longitude and height, which a reader of the specification may expect",
  "UTM keys are not tested here: the unit maths of the bridge covers UTM, the interpolator uses the same conversion"
 ],
